WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.940
Assalam o alaikum.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.840
I'm Hassan Ali Mehdi and this is a Pakistani banknote reader for

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.500
blind people.

00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:12.579
You point your phone at a note and it tells you the value out loud

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:13.299
in Urdu.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.700
Pakistan has about 1.1 million blind people and most daily payments

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.760
are still made in cash.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.280
Our seven notes differ only a little in size so a blind person usually

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.580
has to ask a stranger which note is which,

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:28.800
and trust the answer.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.339
Mixing up a 500 and a 5000 costs 4,500 rupees.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.339
The reader is just a webpage.

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:41.539
It reads the camera 4 times a second and speaks the value in Urdu

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.600
or English with a recorded voice.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.539
So it works even on phones that have no Urdu speech.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.719
It stays silent when it is unsure because saying a wrong amount

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.199
is worse than saying nothing.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.839
Once loaded, it works offline, it also vibrates a code and it

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:58.019
works with screen readers.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.560
I trained on a public dataset of 3,600 real phone photos,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.760
7 notes front and back, many folded or half visible.

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:12.439
I added a no note class with more than 1000 everyday photos so

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:14.280
the model learns when to stay quiet.

00:01:15.559 --> 00:01:18.679
Before trusting any number I checked whether the test photos were

00:01:18.559 --> 00:01:19.200
really new.

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:25.700
I hashed every photo and found that 339 of the 402 test photos

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:30.179
in a second public currency dataset are near copies of the first

00:01:30.059 --> 00:01:30.620
one.

00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:33.519
Only 63 were genuinely new.

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:37.179
Without this check my external test would have looked perfect and

00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:37.799
meant nothing.

00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:44.340
Training images are changed on the GPU every time they are seen:

00:01:44.219 --> 00:01:47.079
any angle, too close, half out of frame,

00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:49.719
fingers over the note, dim or yellow light,

00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:50.420
and blur.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:54.700
No mirror flips because the camera never sees a mirrored note.

00:01:55.979 --> 00:02:02.079
I compared a CNN trained from scratch with two phone networks pre

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:04.859
trained on ImageNet: MobileNet version 3,

00:02:04.739 --> 00:02:07.280
and the new MobileNet version 4.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:11.080
On photos from the same dataset, all three are almost perfect.

00:02:11.479 --> 00:02:14.379
On photos taken by other people the difference shows:

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:17.819
transfer learning holds up far better.

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:21.199
I deployed MobileNet version 3 Large,

00:02:21.079 --> 00:02:24.199
the best on validation and also the fastest.

00:02:25.579 --> 00:02:27.280
Here are the honest numbers.

00:02:27.859 --> 00:02:32.280
100% on unseen photos from the same dataset but 92% on photos

00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:33.180
by other people.

00:02:33.759 --> 00:02:37.099
And on those photos, it never said a wrong amount.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.139
The first number mostly shows how alike the dataset photos are.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.900
The others are what a real user would get.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.139
The most important design decision is when to speak.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.419
On ordinary validation photos, the model is almost never wrong.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:54.199
So any threshold looks safe.

00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:59.080
So I calibrated on a stress test: every validation photo four times.

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:04.340
The app speaks only above 0.68 confidence and only when three

00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:05.500
frames in a row agree.

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:08.240
Here is a live demo.

00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:13.639
I open the site on my phone, tap start camera and hold a note in

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:14.180
front of it.

00:03:14.699 --> 00:03:18.120
It reads several frames per second and speaks the value with nothing

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:18.599
installed.

00:03:19.979 --> 00:03:20.599
Its limits.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.580
The training photos only show a few physical notes,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.319
per value.

00:03:24.719 --> 00:03:28.460
It knows the current note design but not the State Bank's new series.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.400
It reads one note at a time and it does not detect counterfeits.

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:38.939
Next, worn notes counting a whole handful and test with blind users

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:40.300
is what's coming next.

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:42.800
Everything is open.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:44.620
The website, the code, the weights,

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:46.879
the data splits are all linked.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.120
Thank you for watching.
